course: dictionary
lesson: 500
title: Asparagus: a vegetable and a plant
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Asparagus is a noun, so it names a thing. Look at the clues in the sentence to find out which thing. «Carrots and beetroot are roots, celery and asparagus are stems, lettuce and spinach are leaves, broccoli and cauliflower are flowers, and peas and beans are seeds.» The words are grouped by plant parts, and asparagus sits in the stems group.
explain: Context also tells you what asparagus looks like and how we eat it. It has long green, white or purple stalks with pointed tips, and it is eaten cooked. Phrases such as «asparagus spears» and «a bunch of asparagus» show how we talk about it.
explain: Notice that asparagus is uncountable: we say some asparagus, not two asparagus. To count it, we count the spears instead, as in «two asparagus spears».
fact: Asparagus is a noun. It can mean the vegetable you eat or the plant it grows from.
fact: Asparagus has long green, white or purple stalks with pointed tips.
fact: We eat asparagus cooked.
fact: An «asparagus spear» is one stalk, and «a bunch of asparagus» is several stalks tied together.
fact: Asparagus is uncountable, so we use words like some or a bunch of, not numbers like two.
exercise: In «Carrots and beetroot are roots, celery and asparagus are stems», which group is asparagus in? | Roots | Leaves | *Stems | Flowers
exercise: What does asparagus mean? | A small fruit that grows on trees | A type of bird | *A vegetable with long green, white or purple stalks, eaten cooked | A kind of sweet
exercise: Which words describe several asparagus stalks tied together? | Asparagus seeds | An asparagus spear | A stem of asparagus fruit | *A bunch of asparagus
test: In «celery and asparagus are stems», which plant part is asparagus? | *Stem | Leaf | Seed | Root
test: How is asparagus usually eaten? | Raw, straight from the plant | It is never eaten | Only as a drink | *Cooked
exam: Which sentence uses asparagus correctly? | We bought three asparagus. | We bought an asparagus of six. | *We bought some asparagus. | We bought asparagus twos.
